      "why": "The armored red block in the top-left corner (R1C1) can never be hit where it is. The blue magnet at R1C5 is the way out: when it leaves to the right it drags the first block behind it along the top row, and once R1C3 and R1C4 have gone, that first block is the armored one. It lands in R1C5, straight above the clockwise spinner R at R3C5, which still points up and cracks it; then it can leave. So the top row has to be cleared in the right order first (R at R1C3 leaves and turns the spinner Y at R1C4 to point up, then Y leaves), and the rammer must still be pointing up when the shell arrives - its two neighbours, Y at R3C6 and P at R4C5, can leave at once, but each one turns it. Every winning line uses this pull-then-ram (checked exhaustively); only 2 of the 6 legal first moves keep the board solvable.",
